# Project-enforced install hardening (CWE-829, untrusted lifecycle-code execution).
#
# The resolved devDependency graph contains exactly one package that declares an
# install script: unrs-resolver, a non-optional dependency of jest-resolve, whose
# postinstall delegates to napi-postinstall. That fallback can reach for a package
# manager, the network or a child process when a prebuilt native binding is not
# already present, so it must never run unattended.
#
# unrs-resolver cannot be removed (jest-resolve requires it outright) and every
# published version declares the script, so the hazard cannot be resolved by
# version selection - it has to be switched off at install time. Documenting the
# `--ignore-scripts` flag on a command line is not enough: a plain `npm install`
# or `npm ci` without it would still execute the script. Setting the policy here
# makes it the project default for every install, in every checkout and every
# pipeline, whether or not the operator remembers the flag.
#
# npm runs no lifecycle script during installation while this is set. It does NOT
# affect `npm run <script>`, `npm test` or `npm start`, which always execute their
# named script (npm documents this exception explicitly), so all nine scripts in
# package.json keep working unchanged.
#
# The platform-specific native binding unrs-resolver needs is delivered as an
# ordinary optionalDependency and is installed from the registry, not built by the
# script, so Jest resolves normally with scripts disabled.
#
# Deliberately NOT used as remedies here: a preinstall/postinstall hook of our own
# (it would add exactly the execution surface this setting removes) and
# `npm audit fix --force` (it proposes regressing jest by many major versions).
ignore-scripts=true
